# Suppressions must name the control and the reason. An entry without a reason
# is a finding someone hid, and review should treat it as one. Everything not
# listed here fails the build.

# AVD-AWS-0040 / AVD-AWS-0041 (EKS), AVD-AZU-0041 (AKS), AVD-GCP-0061 (GKE) —
# the same decision on three clouds: the API server is reachable from the
# internet in dev and stg, because restricting it needs a bastion or a VPN this
# platform does not provision. Policy CLU-01 asserts the public endpoint is
# disabled in prd, and that assertion is unit tested in
# compliance/policies/kubernetes_test.rego. Narrowing dev and stg is a matter of
# supplying the operator's CIDR ranges, not of changing this code.
AVD-AWS-0040
AVD-AWS-0041
AVD-AZU-0041
AVD-GCP-0061

# AVD-AWS-0104 — nodes need outbound internet for image pulls, OS patching and
# cluster registration, and reach it through the NAT gateway. Egress is already
# restricted by port; the destination cannot be narrowed further without pinning
# registry and API addresses that the providers do not publish.
AVD-AWS-0104

# AVD-AWS-0132 — buckets are encrypted with SSE-KMS under the AWS-managed key.
# A customer-managed key is the stricter option, but it needs a key policy
# granting the service principals that write these objects, which has to be
# validated against a live account before it ships. Tracked, not dismissed: the
# data is encrypted, just not under our own key.
AVD-AWS-0132
